------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART B — WHAT IS NOT LICENSED HERE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The author does not own, and does not license, any Far Cry 3 material. Nothing
in this repository grants you any right to use it.

Excluded from Part A, and remaining the property of their respective owners:

  1. Far Cry 3, the Dunia 2 engine, and every asset inside the game — models,
     textures, meshes, materials, animations, audio, data files, and the object
     library. Ubisoft Entertainment and its licensors own these, and they are
     protected by copyright and trademark law.

  2. The images in the `thumbnails/` folder. Each one is a picture of a Far Cry
     3 prop, rendered at runtime by Far Cry 3's own object renderer while the
     author was using his own legally purchased copy of the game. Rendering does
     not create a new, author-owned artwork: the underlying prop is Ubisoft's,
     so these files are Ubisoft-derived material. The author claims no copyright
     in them, grants no license to them, and could not.

  3. Any game archive (`patch.dat`, `patch.fat`, `common.dat`, `common.fat`),
     extracted fragment, `.xbt`/`.dds` texture, or `.fc3map` file belonging to
     the game, whether or not it appears in discussions here.

  4. Third-party tools and projects referenced in the documentation — including
     the decompiled Far Cry 3 in-game editor source the author builds on, and
     any mod from Nexus Mods or ModDB. Those have their own authors and their
     own terms; this license says nothing about them.

Why the folder is published at all, and what that means for you:

  `thumbnails/` is here so that other Far Cry 3 modders can see the shape of a
  working icon set and compare it against their own. It is meant for private
  study, personal backup, and NonCommercial modding on top of your own copy of
  the game.

  The author is not certain that republishing engine-rendered game assets is
  permitted by Ubisoft's end-user licence, and a repository is not a legal
  opinion. If you redistribute, host, mirror, or bundle these images anywhere,
  you do it on your own judgement and your own risk. If Ubisoft or a rights
  holder asks for this material to be removed, the author will remove it, and
  you should do the same in your copies.

  The path this project actually recommends is generating your own set from your
  own installation, so that nothing Ubisoft-derived ever has to pass through a
  download. README.md documents how.

